      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Neither framework solely possesses an absolute moral advantage, as both address justice but with fundamentally different ethical goals. The debate between their soundness often hinges on whether one prioritizes proportional culpability and deterrence (retributive) or mending harm and fostering accountability (restorative). In the context of modern social justice, restorative justice is frequently favored for its potential to empower marginalized voices and address systemic harms more directly than traditional retribution. Ultimately, most contemporary scholars view the most ethical approach as a principled synthesis that limits excessive punishment while expanding the reach of restorative practices.",
